### Deliberate interpretation and implementation choices

| Issue                                         | Explicit treatment                                                                                          |
| --------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Biological inevitability language             | Preserved as Sher's framing, not used as a mathematical guarantee                                           |
| Search-space versus solution-space vocabulary | Retained; the mapper connects encoded candidates to evaluated consequences                                  |
| Reciprocal graph distance                     | Described as the source objective; executable examples explicitly use MSE instead                           |
| Reversed camouflage color sentence            | Use 0 = white and 1 = green, matching the detailed example and figure                                       |
| Algorithm-family descriptions                 | Presented as the chapter's emphasis, not exhaustive contemporary definitions                                |
| Trees versus graphs                           | Tuple examples support trees only; broader source discussion is not silently implemented                    |
| Memetic expression domain                     | Fix E = 1 and bound C and D so the tangent example remains finite                                           |
| Local tuning and inheritance                  | Keep the two decisions distinct; the controlled comparison does not breed offspring                         |
| GES, novelty, and archive roles               | Clearly marked as our remaster framework and additions                                                      |
| Verification                                  | Independent deterministic reference calculations plus in-notebook assertions; no claimed Livebook execution |

**End of Chapter 3.**
